    "reply": {
      "changeSets": {
        "1": {
          "ARC-REV-01": {
            "evidence": [],
            "rationale": "The blocking decision is in Tier 2. Accounts.ensureCanBorrow, ensureCanHold and ensureCanRenew are called from Loans.borrow, Holds.place and Loans.renew. Tier 3 and Tier 4 only call use cases and views: UseCases.standing and renew, Views.standing and Views.members call Accounts.isBlocked, and the CLI renders the result. The change also removes the CLI's own fines-minus-payments calculation and its `>= 5.00m` check, so a domain rule that sat in Tier 4 now sits in Tier 2. The only Tier 3 restatement is the threshold in message text, which is scored under ARC-REV-03.",
            "score": 2
          },
          "ARC-REV-02": {
            "evidence": [],
            "rationale": "The change adds no effect below Tier 4. It removes the CLI's direct `ports.Store.Load()` and `ports.Store.Save`, and its direct call to `Loans.renew (ports.Clock())`. Renew now goes through `UseCases.renew`, which uses `commit`. The new standing use case goes through `query`. Tier 4 presentation no longer re-enters Tier 2. The existing ports are used, and no new port is needed.",
            "score": 2
          },
          "ARC-REV-03": {
            "evidence": [
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Views.fs",
                "line": 158,
                "quote": "| BorrowingBlocked(MemberId m, owed) -> $\"member {m} owes {money owed}; borrowing is blocked until it is below 5.00\""
              },
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Views.fs",
                "line": 159,
                "quote": "| HoldsBlocked(MemberId m, owed) -> $\"member {m} owes {money owed}; holds are blocked until it is below 5.00\""
              },
              {
                "file": "src/Lending.Application/Views.fs",
                "line": 160,
                "quote": "| RenewalsBlocked(MemberId m, owed) -> $\"member {m} owes {money owed}; renewals are blocked until it is below 5.00\""
              }
            ],
            "rationale": "The threshold has one authoritative definition, `Policy.blockingBalance`, which Accounts.isBlocked references. The Tier 3 error messages copy the literal 5.00 three times instead of taking it from Policy or from the error value. The copies agree today, but they can drift if Policy changes. The change also removes the old duplicate `5.00m` in the CLI.",
            "score": 1
          }
        },
        "2": {
          "ARC-REV-01": {
            "evidence": [],
            "rationale": "The per-member-type limit is decided in Tier 2: `Policy.blockingBalance memberType` and `Accounts.blockingLimit`, used by `isBlocked`. Tier 3 only formats the limit it is given. The `Standard` fallback in blockingLimit for a missing member is also in Tier 2, and callers look the member up first, so it does not conflict with anything.",
            "score": 2
          },
          "ARC-REV-02": {
            "evidence": [],
            "rationale": "The change is pure Tier 1/2 and message-formatting changes. It adds no effects, no I/O and no clock reads, and it leaves port usage unchanged.",
            "score": 2
          },
          "ARC-REV-03": {
            "evidence": [],
            "rationale": "The limit is defined once, in `Policy.blockingBalance` by member type. `isBlocked` and the error values use it through `blockingLimit`. The error carries `limit`, and Views formats `{money limit}`, which removes the three hard-coded `5.00` literals from the previous change. The decision document is updated in step with the code.",
            "score": 2
          }
        }
      },
